<=n<=10. Each such n gives one distinct valid x=(120-n^2)^2, so there are 11 values.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_167"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2006_AMC_10A_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_167","cnm_168"],"id":"count-integer-outputs","label":"Count the possible integer outputs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-integer-outputs":["math:radical-equations","cnm_h020","math:bijective-counting"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_h020","math:bijective-counting","cnm_167","cnm_168"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:3e78afc28d0639beefbf194b"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. -x","reason":"Since 1-(x-1)/x=1/x, the radicand equals x/(1/x)=x^2. Its principal square root is |x|, which equals -x because x<0.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_167"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2006_AMC_10B_Q07","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_156","cnm_167","cnm_008"],"id":"simplify-radicand-and-use-sign","label":"Simplify inside the square root","methodDirectNodeIds":{"simplify-radicand-and-use-sign":["cnm_162","cnm_168"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_162","cnm_168","cnm_156","cnm_167","cnm_008"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:3e78afc28d0639beefbf194b"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, -64","reason":"Equal radicands give t² - 5t - 24 = (t - 8)(t + 3) = 0. Since t ≥ 0, t = 8, so x = ±8. Both satisfy the original equation, and their product is -64.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_167"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2011_AMC_10B_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_008","cnm_167","cnm_168"],"id":"absolute-value-substitution","label":"Solve for the absolute value first","methodDirectNodeIds":{"absolute-value-substitution":["math:radical-equations","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_224","cnm_008","cnm_167","cnm_168"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:3e78afc28d0639beefbf194b"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, -226","reason":"The successive domains are D₁=(-∞,1], D₂=[3,4], D₃=[-7,0], D₄={16}, and D₅={25-16²}={-231}. D₆ is empty because a principal square root cannot equal -231, and all later domains remain empty. Thus N+c=5-231=-226.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_167"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2011_AMC_12A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_074","cnm_167","cnm_168"],"id":"track-successive-domains","label":"Track the admissible inputs at each composition","methodDirectNodeIds":{"track-successive-domains":["cnm_h028","math:function-composition-iteration"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h028","math:function-composition-iteration","cnm_074","cnm_167","cnm_168"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:3e78afc28d0639beefbf194b"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, extraneous candidate x=-9; the true root is x=26","reason":"Squaring x+4=5√(x+10) gives (x-26)(x+9)=0. At x=-9 the original left side is 1-6=-5, whereas x=26 gives 6-1=5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_167"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1953_AMC_12_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_167","cnm_040","cnm_148"],"id":"solution:isolate-and-square:dcb474b47e30","label":"Clear the denominator and square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"isolate-and-square":["math:radical-equations","cnm_168","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","cnm_168","cnm_224","cnm_167","cnm_040","cnm_148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:3e78afc28d0639beefbf194b"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 25sqrt(5)","reason":"Factoring gives 3*4^(x-1)=24, hence 2^(2x-2)=8 and x=5/2. Therefore (2x)^x=5^(5/2)=25sqrt(5).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_167"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1958_AMC_12_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_015","cnm_042","cnm_167"],"id":"factor-and-compare-powers","label":"Factor the common power","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-and-compare-powers":["math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h042","cnm_h039"]},"nodeIds":["math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h042","cnm_h039","cnm_015","cnm_042","cnm_167"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:3e78afc28d0639beefbf194b"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B. r approaches 1/sqrt(6)","reason":"L(m)=-sqrt(6+m), so for m≠0, r=[sqrt(6+m)-sqrt(6-m)]/m=2/[sqrt(6+m)+sqrt(6-m)]. This approaches 1/sqrt(6).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_167"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":["The original quotient is undefined at m=0; the answer concerns its limit, not substitution into that quotient."],"questionId":"1969_AMC_12_Q35","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_147","cnm_167","cnm_195"],"id":"rationalize-the-difference","label":"Remove the vanishing difference","methodDirectNodeIds":{"rationalize-the-difference":["math:elementary-function-limits","cnm_220","cnm_173"]},"nodeIds":["math:elementary-function-limits","cnm_220","cnm_173","cnm_147","cnm_167","cnm_195"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:3e78afc28d0639beefbf194b"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. None of these; x^(−1/2)=√2/4","reason":"The logarithms successively give log₃(log₂x)=1, log₂x=3, and x=8. Hence x^(−1/2)=1/√8=√2/4, which is not listed.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_167"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1983_AMC_12_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h046","cnm_1